# Committed, non-secret production defaults.
#
# Read this before adding anything here.
#
# Next.js loads this file for production builds AND in the production runtime,
# and it is the wrong place for a placeholder.
#
# The precedence, verified against @next/env 16.3.1 on 2026-09-14 rather than
# assumed: a name ALREADY PRESENT in the environment is never overwritten. So
# Vercel always wins for the names it defines, and this file only ever supplies
# the names Vercel does not.
#
# That makes the dangerous case the opposite of the one previously described
# here. The risk is not that a value here overrides a real one in Vercel — it
# cannot. The risk is that a name documented here with an EMPTY assignment, and
# not set in Vercel, arrives in the running process as '' rather than as
# undefined. Code that falls back with `??` keeps the blank, because nullish
# coalescing only fires on null/undefined.
#
# That has now broken production twice: #19 (NUTRIENT_ALLOWED_MIME_TYPES, which
# took out every document route) and the inbound Twilio webhook, which verified
# every signature against an empty URL and so answered 403 to every inbound
# message. Fall back on falsiness, not nullishness.
#
# This file used to carry placeholder database, NextAuth, Google OAuth and
# Nutrient credentials, added to avoid "Invalid URL" errors during builds. A
# placeholder connection string here can be read in preference to the real Neon
# URL configured in Vercel, which is reason enough not to keep one.
#
# They were also unnecessary: lib/prisma.ts builds its client lazily through a
# Proxy, so nothing connects to a database during the build.
#
# What these placeholders did NOT cause is the production outage that is easy to
# blame on them. Every query failing against 127.0.0.1:5432 was a separate bug —
# the Prisma adapter was handed a `pg.Pool` it could not recognise under pnpm and
# died at protocol startup, reporting a host that was never involved. That is
# explained at length in lib/prisma.ts. Both faults pointed at localhost, which
# is precisely why they were mistaken for one.
#
# Rule: only add a value here if it is not a secret AND it is correct in
# production. Anything else belongs in Vercel's environment settings alone.

# Nutrient Viewer CDN version. Not a secret, and this is the real value, so it is
# safe for it to win. Loaded by app/layout.tsx from
# https://cdn.cloud.pspdfkit.com/pspdfkit-web@<version>/nutrient-viewer.js
NUTRIENT_VIEWER_VERSION="1.20.0"

# Which Nutrient backend this deployment talks to, and the limits it imposes.
# Resolved and validated in lib/nutrient-config.ts; see the doc comment there
# for why each of these is configuration rather than a constant.
#
# "dws" is the hosted API at api.nutrient.io. "document-engine" is a customer's
# own server -- note there is no client for it yet, so setting it here fails
# loudly at startup rather than misbehaving quietly.
NUTRIENT_TARGET=dws

# An origin only: no path, no trailing slash. Call sites append their own paths.
# Left unset so the hosted default applies. Required when NUTRIENT_TARGET is
# "document-engine", which has no well-known address.
#
# Supersedes two older names that meant different things and are still read as
# fallbacks: NUTRIENT_API_BASE_URL held an origin *and* the documents path,
# NUTRIENT_API_BASE_URL_ROOT held an origin only. NUTRIENT_API_BASE_URL is still
# set in Vercel; either is reduced to an origin, so it stays harmless, but new
# deployments should set this one instead.
NUTRIENT_BASE_URL=

# Upload ceilings. Left unset so the defaults apply, which are the hosted API's
# own published limits.
#
# Two other ceilings sit above whatever is set here and neither is ours to
# change: the hosted API refuses a file over 100MB with a 413, and a Vercel
# function accepts a request body up to 100MB. Configuring more than the hosted
# cap is rejected at startup rather than accepted, because raising it cannot
# work. A self-hosted Document Engine has no such cap.
NUTRIENT_MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES=
NUTRIENT_ALLOWED_MIME_TYPES=

# How long to wait for one backend call. The Processor API has no async mode --
# OCR and redaction return the finished document in the response body -- so this
# has to allow for a slow job rather than a fast round trip. Defaults to 120s.
NUTRIENT_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=

# Twilio SMS notifications. TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID and TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN are secrets
# and are deliberately left empty here — set them in Vercel's environment
# settings, not in this file. TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN also doubles as the inbound
# webhook signing key, so rotating it must happen alongside re-verifying
# app/api/webhooks/twilio/route.ts still receives messages.
TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=

# The program's phone number. Not a secret — it is published at
# https://jonaddams.com/sms and filed with the A2P 10DLC campaign — so the real
# value belongs here, where it can be read and checked against the filing. The
# Vercel copy is encrypted and cannot be read back to confirm it is right.
#
# It does NOT "win over Vercel", as this comment used to claim: Vercel defines
# TWILIO_PHONE_NUMBER for production, and a name already in the environment is
# never overwritten by this file. Vercel's value is the one that runs. So the
# two must agree, and this file's copy is documentation rather than the source
# of truth — if they ever diverge, the deployment uses Vercel's.
#
# This must stay in step with three other places, because a carrier reviewer
# compares them: the number published on /sms, the number filed with the
# campaign, and the number the app displays on its opt-in screen. That screen
# renders this value through formatProgramNumber(), so a stale value here tells
# a user to text a number that cannot receive, and lib/twilio.ts sends every
# outbound message from it.
#
# It held the trial number +17372583742 until 2026-09-03. That number died when
# the account was upgraded; do not reintroduce it.
TWILIO_PHONE_NUMBER=+12692925337

# Only needed when the URL Twilio was configured to POST to differs from what
# the app sees as request.url — e.g. behind a proxy or a rewritten host. Twilio
# signs the URL it was configured with, not the one the request arrives on, so
# a mismatch here breaks inbound signature verification. Leave unset unless
# that mismatch is confirmed.
TWILIO_WEBHOOK_URL=

# Document processing. Two variables, both secrets, both documented as comments
# rather than as empty assignments — see the note on the BetterAuth block for
# why an empty assignment here is not the harmless documentation it looks like.
#
#   NUTRIENT_PROCESSOR_API_KEY  Opens the Processor API — POST /build — and
#                               nothing on the Viewer surface. It is a DIFFERENT
#                               key from NUTRIENT_VIEWER_API_KEY, and the two
#                               are not interchangeable in either direction:
#                               each answers 403 Forbidden to the other's
#                               requests. Both start with pdf_live_ and are the
#                               same length, so confirm which is which in the
#                               Nutrient dashboard rather than by eye.
#
#   CRON_SECRET                 Authenticates the scheduled job sweep at
#                               /api/cron/sweep-jobs. Vercel Cron sends it as
#                               `Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET`. The sweep
#                               performs billed work, so without this the
#                               endpoint refuses every request (503) rather than
#                               running openly.
